# --- build products ---
#
# bin\ is generated entirely by build.ps1 and holds nothing written by hand: the
# binaries are ~43 MB each and change at every build.
bin/

# Outside bin\ too, because a "go build" without -o leaves the executable in the
# project folder and that has happened. The .exe~ is created by Windows when
# rebuilding over a running process: it renames the old one instead of deleting
# it.
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.pdb

# The .syso is the resource holding the executable's icon. build.ps1 regenerates
# it at every build with `go run ./cmd/pat-icon`: it derives entirely from the
# drawing, which is code instead, and it is deterministic. Versioning it would
# mean keeping two copies of the same thing aligned.
*.syso

# --- test outputs ---
#
# Raw streams from the spikes, muxer clips, audio recordings, and the monitor's
# logs when it is started by hand.
*.h264
*.264
*.mp4
*.ogg
*.wav
*.pcm
*.log
*.err
*.pid

# Go test binaries and reports.
*.test
*.out
*.prof

# --- configuration and state ---
#
# The configuration file holds the password hash and, if it has been set, the
# Tailscale auth key. In service it lives in %APPDATA%\PAT Monitor\ and not here,
# but one -config pointed inside the project is enough to leave one behind.
config.yaml
config.yaml.tmp
config.local.yaml

# The Tailscale node's state: node keys and TLS certificate. Copying it
# elsewhere means cloning the monitor's identity.
tsnet/
*.state

# --- local environment ---
go.work
go.work.sum
.claude/settings.local.json
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# The raw webrtc-internals dump: it holds the real addresses of the home network
# — the public IPv4 and the IPv6 of the individual devices — because ICE
# candidates are addresses. On a public repository they would be permanent.
#
# Nothing useful is lost: the measurement that matters is already distilled into
# baselines/latency.txt, which is versioned. The dump is the rough sheet.
webrtc_internals_dump.txt

# --- deliberately NOT ignored ---
#
# baselines/capture-*.txt and baselines/latency.txt look like leftovers and are
# not: they are the regularity of the capture and the latency measured when
# everything was working, that is, the only way of answering "has it got worse?".
# A measurement taken later, when the suspicion has already arisen, has nothing
# to compare itself with.

# Service logs. In service they live in %APPDATA%\PAT Monitor\log\; they end up
# here only if the monitor was started with -config pointed into the project.
# They hold the viewers' IP addresses, so they must not be published, and they
# are not a reference measurement: they are the chronicle of one night, useful
# while the fault is fresh.
log/

# The workbench: what is needed **in order to redo** the measurements in
# baselines/, not the measurements. Two kinds of thing, and neither of them
# belongs in a repository.
#
#   ced-tiny-f16.gguf     the unquantised weights, somebody else's work — CED,
#                         Apache-2.0, © Xiaomi. They are what parity with the
#                         oracle is measured against, and the fingerprint is in
#                         baselines/ced.txt, because git does not reach here.
#   config.json           how the upstream model declares its front-end.
#   sweep/                the scores of the sweep over the three public
#                         datasets: one row per clip, one column per class. Six
#                         megabytes holding fifty minutes of work, and
#                         `pat-sounds judge` reads them back without the twelve
#                         gigabytes.
#
# What gets shipped is not here: it is internal/ced/ced-tiny-q8_0.gguf, embedded
# with go:embed like libopus.wasm, and its notice is in licenses/.
workbench/

# The release signing key. The public half is a constant in
# internal/update/pubkey.go; this is the private one, and it exists on one
# machine. Committed, it would be worthless: the whole point is that whoever
# takes the GitHub account cannot sign a release.
*.key

# The assembled release: the staged folder, the zip and its signature. Built by
# build.ps1 -Release from what is already in the repository, and uploaded to a
# GitHub release rather than committed.
dist/
